WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.124
Secure subprocess usage means using Python to launch external programs in
a controlled, explicit, and reviewable way.

2
00:00:07.124 --> 00:00:16.857
The subprocess module is powerful because it lets Python code start child
processes, pass inputs, capture outputs, wait for completion, and react to
return codes.

3
00:00:16.857 --> 00:00:28.847
That power appears in web applications, APIs, command-line tools,
automation scripts, background workers, scheduled tasks, build systems,
data pipelines, and internal administrative utilities.

4
00:00:28.847 --> 00:00:34.419
Subprocess usage is different from calling a Python library or using a
platform API directly.

5
00:00:34.419 --> 00:00:40.556
A library call usually stays inside the Python process and follows
familiar application control flow.

6
00:00:40.556 --> 00:00:50.360
A child process runs as a separate program with its own parsing rules,
input streams, output streams, environment, working directory,
permissions, and lifecycle.

7
00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:57.131
It may read files, write files, use network access, produce diagnostic
output, or continue longer than expected.

8
00:00:57.131 --> 00:01:07.075
That separation creates trust boundaries between Python code, the
operating system, files, environment variables, user-controlled inputs,
and the child process.

9
00:01:07.075 --> 00:01:21.605
A seemingly simple helper can affect confidentiality if it receives
secrets it does not need, integrity if it writes to unintended locations,
availability if it runs without limits, and auditability if output and
errors are logged carelessly.

10
00:01:21.605 --> 00:01:26.472
Subprocess code deserves the same attention as database access or file
handling.

11
00:01:26.472 --> 00:01:33.736
The safest design is often to avoid subprocess entirely when a well-
supported Python library can perform the task safely.

12
00:01:33.736 --> 00:01:54.120
When an external program is genuinely needed, the code should make the
boundary obvious: choose the executable deliberately, pass arguments as
structured values, avoid shell interpretation, validate inputs, control
paths and environment variables, apply least privilege, use timeouts,
handle output safely, and test failure behavior.
